00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.720 align:start position:0%
a curfew couldn't keep protesters in  Minneapolis at home fires on the streets  of a city in its fourth night of unrest  and across America solidarity turned  into aggression all triggered by the  death of George Floyd an unarmed black  man who died whilst in police custody  protesters in Atlanta targeted CNN's  headquarters and in Brooklyn police used  force to break up crowds the anger is  still palpable  despite this announcement earlier in the  day has been charged by the hand of a  County Attorney's Office with murder and  with manslaughter  Chapman could face up to 25 years in  prison if convicted of the most serious  charge third-degree murder the evidence  in this case has played out across the  world but it's still hard to watch  Floyd gasping for breath and begging  chavin to get off his neck for eight  minutes a new footage appears to show  other officers kneeling on him president  Trump struck an empathetic tone after  earlier making divisive comments on  Twitter I understand the hurt I  understand the pain people have really  been through a lot the family of George  is entitled to justice and the people of  Minnesota are entitled to live in safety  Floyd's relatives have welcomed  Chapman's arrest but not the charges  this community wants Derek chavin to  face a more serious murder charge they  also want charges for the other officers  involved and until that happens what  we're seeing here is some people are  simply not willing to stay in and obey  orders  the fate of these officers won't just  matter - George Floyd's family many in  America are appalled by the sight of  another black man dying at the hands of  the police often without consequence and  they are demanding justice  Becky Cottrill Sky News Minneapolis
 

